Across the four stationary JONSWAP seas, the mean normalized vertical error
formed by first averaging the four seas within each seed triplet is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III and
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II, where the quoted spread is the sample standard deviation across the
$\OUValidationStationaryPairs$ seed triplets rather than an interval for the
mean.  The paired OU--III-minus-OU--II difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage point, with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
This paired difference and its interval are the primary statement of effect
size.  The standardized companions, Cohen's $d_z=\OUValidationNormalizedDz$ and
Hedges' corrected $g_z=\OUValidationNormalizedGz$, are reported for
completeness only; at $n=\OUValidationStationaryPairs$ with strong pairing they
are large because the standard deviation of the seed-level differences is both
small and imprecisely estimated, and they should not be read as a further
strengthening of the claim.

\paragraph{The vertical gain does not extend to three dimensions}
This is the clearest negative result of the study and it bounds the
contribution, so it is stated before the ablations.  Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_axes}
gives absolute per-axis and 3D displacement RMS for both filters, so the
magnitude can be read directly rather than inferred from a difference.
OU--III has \emph{higher} 3D displacement error than OU--II in
\OUValidationThreeDHigherCount{} of the \OUValidationThreeDScenarios{}
scenarios, with resolved intervals; the nominal \SI{1.5}{m} sea is
unresolved, its interval spanning zero.  The gap grows with sea state, and it
is horizontal: the vertical column favors OU--III everywhere while the X and Y
columns move the other way.  The additional integral-displacement state and
its anisotropic regularization therefore buy vertical accuracy at a horizontal
cost that the vertical-only score does not show.

Read together with Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_family}, the evidence supports a
stationary vertical claim and no more.  OU--III has resolved vertical
improvements in all four stationary seas, but higher vertical error during the
transition, and higher full-3D error in most scenarios.  Roll and pitch effects
likewise change sign or have intervals spanning zero in most cases.  The
additional OU state provides a clear aggregate \emph{vertical} benefit for this
stationary JONSWAP family; it is not a better three-dimensional wave-state
estimator, and nothing here supports describing it as one.

\paragraph{Second spectral family}
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_pmstokes} repeats the paired comparison on the four
PM--Stokes seas over the same window and the same seed triplets.  The
seed-level mean normalized vertical error is
$\OUValidationPMStokesOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationPMStokesOUIIINormalizedStd\%$
of $H_s$ for OU--III against
$\OUValidationPMStokesOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationPMStokesOUIINormalizedStd\%$
for OU--II, a paired difference of $\OUValidationPMStokesDifference$ percentage
point
($[\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceLow,\OUValidationPMStokesDifferenceHigh]$,
$n=\OUValidationPMStokesPairs$).  Because PM--Stokes adds third-order bound
harmonics, it is a separate prespecified ensemble and is not pooled into the
primary aggregate; it extends the vertical result to a second spectral family
rather than enlarging the confirmatory sample.

\paragraph{Wave direction, and the limit of the travel-sense evidence}
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_direction} replaces the single deterministic window of
Table~\ref{tab:sim_results_rms_dir_basic} with a ten-seed statement against the
generator azimuth of each record.  The axis result is strong: pooled over the
eight stationary seas the absolute axial error of the circular-mean estimate is
\OUValidationDirectionAbsError{} degrees and no scenario exceeds
\OUValidationDirectionWorstAbsError{} degrees, against a sample-wise axial RMS
error of \OUValidationDirectionRMSE{} degrees.  The propagation \emph{axis} is
therefore recovered to a degree or two in the mean, with substantial
sample-to-sample dispersion about it.

Travel sense is now scored against truth rather than against the estimator's
own axis representative.  The directed propagation vector, with the vessel
heading removed, is compared with the physical propagation direction of the
record, which is the generator azimuth plus \SI{180}{\degree}: the earlier
reports could not make that comparison because the sign convention of the
generator was never asserted anywhere, and it is now pinned by a test.  Pooled
over the eight stationary seas the estimator resolves the travel sense
correctly in \OUValidationTravelCorrect\% of samples, incorrectly in
\OUValidationTravelWrong\%, and leaves \OUValidationTravelUnresolved\% below the
confidence and amplitude thresholds, with a pooled directed angular error of
\OUValidationTravelAbsError{} degrees.

The FORWARD/BACKWARD labels the estimator exports remain a gauge quantity and
are not scored.  Running the same sea with the vessel heading rotated by
\SI{180}{\degree} -- identical waves, identical seeds, only the platform turned
around -- leaves heave, three-dimensional displacement and the propagation axis
unchanged while the class shares invert completely, from \SI{95.5}{\percent}
FORWARD to \SI{94.6}{\percent} BACKWARD, because the axis estimator seeds its
representative from the bow.  The same experiment leaves the truth-referenced
correctness rate flat at headings of \num{0}, \num{45} and
\SI{180}{\degree}, which is what establishes that the directed output is
physical while the class label is not.  What remains for
future work is the unimodal limitation of these records: each holds
one nominal heading, so direction changes and multimodal seas are outside what
any of these numbers cover.

The adaptation ablation in Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_adaptation} is also mixed.
Every fixed operating point it uses is listed in
Table~\ref{tab:ou_fixed_points}, so each entry is reproducible from a single
frozen triple.  Relative to FixedNominal, Adaptive reduces normalized vertical
error at both off-nominal extremes: by
$\OUValidationIIHsZeroTwoSevenNominalDifference$ and
$\OUValidationIIHsEightFiveNominalDifference$ percentage points for OU--II and by
$\OUValidationIIIHsZeroTwoSevenNominalDifference$ and
$\OUValidationIIIHsEightFiveNominalDifference$ points for OU--III at $H_s=0.27$ and
\SI{8.5}{m}, respectively; all four paired bootstrap intervals exclude zero.
That is the intended behavior.  A single frozen point calibrated at
$H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ is badly mismatched at either extreme: wave energy scales
with $H_s^{2}$, so $\SI{0.27}{m}$ and $\SI{8.5}{m}$ each differ from the
nominal sea by a factor of about $31$, roughly \emph{one and a half} orders of
magnitude rather than two.  Over that range the online tuner recovers most of
the mismatch, but not uniformly.  Measuring recovery as the share of the
FixedNominal-to-FixedOracle gap that adaptation closes, OU--III recovers
\pct{\OUValidationIIIHsZeroTwoSevenRecovered} of it at $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$ but
only \pct{\OUValidationIIIHsEightFiveRecovered} at \SI{8.5}{m}
(Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_adaptation}).  That the tuner ``removes most of the
mismatch'' is therefore defensible as an aggregate statement over the tested
range, and not as a claim about either endpoint separately.

\paragraph{Which channel earns the benefit}
Adaptive-versus-FixedNominal moves $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, and $r_S$ together,
and the deployed law ties the third to the first two, so on its own it cannot
establish that joint OU adaptation is what helps.
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_channels} completes the $2\times2$ factorial described in
Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval}, and the answer is one-sided.  At
$H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$, letting $r_S$ adapt while $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ stay
frozen improves on FixedNominal by
$\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenRSOnlyDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenRSOnlyLow,\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenRSOnlyHigh]$),
whereas letting $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ adapt with $r_S$ frozen changes it by
only $\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenOUOnlyDifference$ points
($[\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenOUOnlyLow,\OUValidationChannelHsZeroTwoSevenOUOnlyHigh]$).
The same ordering holds at \SI{8.5}{m}
($\OUValidationChannelHsEightFiveRSOnlyDifference$ against
$\OUValidationChannelHsEightFiveOUOnlyDifference$ points) and at
\SI{4.0}{m}.  The $r_S$ channel accounts for the entire adaptation benefit;
the OU channel accounts for none of it.

The factorial says something further that the Adaptive-versus-Fixed comparison
could not.  Reading the first two columns of
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_channels} against each other, adapting $r_S$ alone is not
merely most of full adaptation but is \emph{better} than it at
$H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$, at \SI{8.5}{m}, and over the transition, and is worse only
at the two smallest seas.  Since the two configurations differ solely in
whether $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ track the sea, the estimation jitter those two
channels inject is a net cost wherever the frozen values are not badly
mismatched -- the same jitter identified below as the reason Adaptive loses to
a well-matched frozen point.  We report this as an observation on the tested
scenarios rather than a tuning recommendation: the study computes no interval
for that particular contrast, and choosing a configuration from these scored
errors would break the frozen, sensor-only protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval}.

This narrows the contribution in a way the earlier framing did not.  What the
vertical result demonstrates is the value of \emph{adaptive integral-state
regularization}, not of joint OU adaptation.  It is consistent with the
mechanism identified in Sec.~\ref{sec:ou-robustness}, where the accelerometer's
direct \SI{200}{Hz} observation of $\vct{a}_w$ leaves that gain near saturation
while $r_S$ governs the unobserved low-frequency channel.  $\tau$ and
$\sigma_{aw}$ are not thereby idle -- they set the prior the estimator
integrates, they drive $r_S$ through the deployed law, and
Sec.~\ref{sec:ou-robustness} shows $\sigma_{aw}$ matters for attitude -- but
the evidence does not support claiming that full three-parameter adaptation is
necessary for the vertical improvement, and at the larger seas it is not even
preferable.

Adaptive does not, however, beat either fixed reference at the nominal sea or
over the transition.  At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ sea, FixedNominal and
FixedOracle coincide by construction, and Adaptive is worse than that shared
point by $\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalDifference$ points
($[\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalLow,\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalHigh]$) for
OU--III.  Since both configurations use the same nominal parameter values on
average, the residual gap is the cost of estimating those parameters from
noisy IMU-derived signals rather than reading them from a noise-free
calibration: the online $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, and $r_S$ jitter about the
frozen point, and Sec.~\ref{sec:ou-robustness} shows that displacement error
responds to $r_S$.  During the controlled transition Adaptive is worse than
FixedNominal by $\OUValidationIITransitionNominalDifference$ points
($[\OUValidationIITransitionNominalLow,\OUValidationIITransitionNominalHigh]$)
for OU--II and $\OUValidationIIITransitionNominalDifference$ points
($[\OUValidationIIITransitionNominalLow,\OUValidationIIITransitionNominalHigh]$)
for OU--III, and worse than the endpoint-calibrated FixedOracle by
$\OUValidationIITransitionOracleDifference$ and
$\OUValidationIIITransitionOracleDifference$ points respectively.

Those transition percentages are normalized by the final $H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$,
which the sea does not reach until $780$~s, so they are not comparable with the
stationary $Z/H_s$ entries above.  Table~\ref{tab:ou_transition_segments}
therefore rescores the same runs on the three disjoint intervals of the window,
in absolute metres and against the reference displacement RMS of each interval.
Read that way, the Adaptive penalty is not spread evenly: relative to
FixedNominal it is
$\OUValidationSegmentIIIStartNominalDifference$ points of $Z/Z_{\mathrm{ref}}$
over the pure start sea
($[\OUValidationSegmentIIIStartNominalLow,\OUValidationSegmentIIIStartNominalHigh]$),
$\OUValidationSegmentIIIBlendNominalDifference$ points across the crossfade
($[\OUValidationSegmentIIIBlendNominalLow,\OUValidationSegmentIIIBlendNominalHigh]$),
and $\OUValidationSegmentIIIEndNominalDifference$ points over the pure endpoint
sea
($[\OUValidationSegmentIIIEndNominalLow,\OUValidationSegmentIIIEndNominalHigh]$)
for OU--III.  Comparing the endpoint interval against the stationary
$H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$ row is the closest like-for-like reading the protocol
supports.

Two properties of the protocol limit what that transition result can be said
to show, and both are quantified rather than assumed.  The endpoint-calibrated
reference is favored by the composition of the scoring window, \pct{47} of
which is the pure endpoint sea
(Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval}).  And the aggregate cannot separate estimation
jitter from a genuine lag in following the sea.  The covariance-policy control
of Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_covsync} rules out one specific alternative
explanation.  All three primary modes re-align the posterior
$\mat{P}_{a_w a_w}$ marginal at the adaptation cadence, so the ablation is
already matched on that factor; switching the re-alignment off changes
normalized vertical error by at most
$\OUValidationCovSyncWorstDifference$ points
($[\OUValidationCovSyncWorstLow,\OUValidationCovSyncWorstHigh]$, worst case
across scenarios, families, and both matched pairs), which is far smaller than
the adaptation gaps above.  The covariance policy is therefore neither a
confound in the ablation nor the source of the Adaptive disadvantage.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_transition} shows one
realization directly.  The conclusion supported by this evidence is that
online tuning improves robustness to large stationary mismatch and costs
accuracy where a well-matched frozen point is already available; attributing
the transition gap specifically to adaptation lag would require a protocol
that holds the scored sea-state composition fixed while varying only the
adaptation rate.

